1
00:00:00,750 --> 00:00:07,103
In 1331, an abbot set his town's hand mills into his floor.

2
00:00:07,203 --> 00:00:11,560
How does a hand mill end up underfoot? Start your day at dawn.

3
00:00:13,210 --> 00:00:16,112
Dawn. Somebody is hammering on your door.

4
00:00:16,412 --> 00:00:20,340
WALTER THE REEVE: Up! It's a boon day. The whole household, in the lord's field!

5
00:00:20,440 --> 00:00:22,780
Today you work for the lord. For free.

6
00:00:24,080 --> 00:00:28,208
At harvest, every household owed the lord extra days of reaping.

7
00:00:28,308 --> 00:00:33,617
Sickle in one hand, stalks in the other. Cut. Bind. Stack. All day.

8
00:00:33,717 --> 00:00:37,725
Walter the reeve, a neighbour the village chose, notches it on his tally.

9
00:00:38,525 --> 00:00:42,701
The good news? On boon days, the lord had to feed you.

10
00:00:42,801 --> 00:00:46,121
Bread. Cheese. Bacon, if you were lucky. And ale.

11
00:00:46,221 --> 00:00:49,973
Manor records literally sorted the days into 'ale' boons...

12
00:00:50,223 --> 00:00:51,698
...and 'dry' boons.

13
00:00:52,798 --> 00:00:56,144
Sunset. You've gleaned some grain of your own.

14
00:00:56,244 --> 00:01:01,042
So you grind it at home on your hand mill, the quern. What could go wrong?

15
00:01:02,498 --> 00:01:04,942
WALTER THE REEVE: Is that a hand mill? In this house?

16
00:01:05,042 --> 00:01:10,907
On many manors, you had to grind at the lord's mill, and hand him a cut of your grain.

17
00:01:11,007 --> 00:01:15,632
Home grinding dodged his fee. So lords banned hand mills.

18
00:01:16,632 --> 00:01:22,998
In 1331, the abbot of St Albans seized the town's hand mills...

19
00:01:23,098 --> 00:01:27,408
...and set the millstones into the floor of his parlour. As a 'memorial'.

20
00:01:28,158 --> 00:01:32,227
So the abbey walked on the town's querns. Every day.

21
00:01:33,958 --> 00:01:38,705
Fifty years later came the revolt of 1381...

22
00:01:38,805 --> 00:01:42,383
...the townsfolk prised up those stones and smashed them.

23
00:01:42,683 --> 00:01:46,201
Then they handed out the pieces like blessed bread at church.

24
00:01:47,101 --> 00:01:49,992
Back home, you hide the quern under the straw.

25
00:01:50,092 --> 00:01:54,437
Your field. Your flour. Your rules. For tonight.

26
00:01:54,837 --> 00:01:57,661
Next time: you become a Roman gladiator.
